[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## How the rows are executed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `translation/tests/differential.rs` contains one case per row, named
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `phase_b_rowNN_*`. Each case:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. resolves the five exported symbols in **both** `.so`s with `libloading`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (`dlopen`/`dlsym`) — the Rust functions are never called directly, so the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `#[no_mangle] extern "C"` wrappers are part of what is under test;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. redirects file descriptor 1 to a temp file, replays the row's whole input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    batch against the C library, restores fd 1, then does the same for the Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    library;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. compares the two byte strings with `assert_eq`-style exactness.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Replaying inputs in **batches inside one capture** is deliberate: it keeps the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m run fast *and* means every row also exercises repeated/sequenced calls through
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the shared libc `stdout` buffer, which a one-call-per-capture design would not.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two harness details are load-bearing:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * the suite runs under `harness = false`. libtest writes its own progress text
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   to fd 1 from a different thread, and that text lands inside the captured bytes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and produces spurious divergences. The custom runner in `main()` prints only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   while fd 1 is un-redirected.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * each case runs in a `fork()`ed child. A translation bug that dereferences a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pointer the C guards against shows up as `SIGSEGV`; without isolation that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   would kill the runner and hide every other result. Signal deaths are reported
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   as failures.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `cargo test` alone does **not** re-link the `cdylib`, so it is possible to run
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the entire suite against a stale `libdriver.so`. The harness therefore refuses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m to run when `src/lib.rs` or `Cargo.toml` is newer than the `.so`. Use
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `./run_verification.sh` (or `cargo build --release && cargo test --release`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Results
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m All 26 rows pass, under every configuration:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | configuration | result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---------------|--------|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | default features, Rust release cdylib vs C (CMake default, no `-O`) | 58/58 cases pass |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `--no-default-features` (identical: the crate declares no features) | 58/58 cases pass |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust **debug** cdylib vs the same C reference | 58/58 cases pass |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust release cdylib vs C rebuilt with `-O2` | 58/58 cases pass |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust release cdylib vs C rebuilt with `-O3` | 58/58 cases pass |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The last three matter because `bad()` relies on C undefined behaviour
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`(int)` of an out-of-range `double`). Matching at `-O0`, `-O2` and `-O3` shows
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the Rust reproduces the platform's actual `cvttsd2si` semantics rather than an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m artefact of one optimisation level. The alternative C builds are produced with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `gcc` into a temp directory; `c_src/` is never modified.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 58 cases cover 26 `CONFIGS.md` rows + 32 `ERRORS.md` rows (several error rows
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m share a case where the C treats them identically) + 2 reachability proofs + the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m symbol-parity check.
